    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "The filesystem reports 0 available with 100% used. The apt cache is tiny, so something is consuming space elsewhere. I need to find what's taking up space and free it to allow the install.",
  "plan": "Find large files/directories to identify space hogs. Then clean up to free space. Check /tmp, /var, /root, and any large logs.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh /* 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -20\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "df shows 954G used / 0 available, but du of / shows only ~836M used. This large discrepancy indicates either deleted files still held open by processes, or the overlay backing store is genuinely full. I need to investigate what's consuming the backing space.",
  "plan": "Check for deleted-but-open files with lsof, and look at mount options. If a process holds deleted large files, I may be able to free space. Otherwise, I'll try installing to a different location or work around the space issue.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "lsof 2>/dev/null | grep -i deleted | sort -k7 -h | tail -20\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "mount | grep -i overlay\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "df reports 0 available on / but du of top-level dirs shows only ~836M used. This is an overlayfs on a host backing store (/data/docker) that appears full. I need to understand where the 954G is actually going. Let me investigate whether I can write to disk at all and find the real space usage.",
  "plan": "Test writing a file, check inode usage, and look for the real large consumers. The 7z2john.pl only needs Compress::Raw::Lzma which is a small perl module. I may be able to work around the apt space issue by finding alternative installs or freeing space.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "du -sh -x / 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "df -i /\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "touch /test_write && echo WRITE_OK && rm /test_write\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "There's a key discrepancy: `du -x /` shows only 836M used, but `df /` reports 954G used / 0 available. This is an overlayfs backed by the host's /data/docker storage shared with the whole host. The host backing filesystem is full. However, root can still write to reserved blocks (test_write succeeded). apt refuses because it does a statvfs space check that reports 0 available. I need to verify how much I can actually write, and check whether the perl Lzma module and 7z are truly needed/available. Let me investigate before deciding how to install.",
  "plan": "1. Test writing a larger file to see real writable space. 2. Check if Compress::Raw::Lzma is already available. 3. Check 7z2john.pl's dependencies. 4. Look for any existing 7z binary. Based on results, decide whether to retry apt (possibly bypassing its size check) or work around.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmptest bs=1M count=5 2>&1; rm -f /tmptest\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "perl -MCompress::Raw::Lzma -e 'print \"LZMA_OK\\n\"' 2>&1\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "head -30 /app/john/run/7z2john.pl\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls /app/john/run/ | grep -i 7z; which 7z 7za p7zip 2>&1\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Key finding: `df /` reports 0 available (954G used) but `du -x /` shows only 836M actually in this overlay, and I CAN write files (touch and a 5MB dd both succeeded). This means the host backing filesystem is full but root still has reserved blocks to write into. apt refused to install because its statvfs space check sees 0 available. The .deb files I need are small (~1.8MB), so the real fix is to bypass apt's space check — download the .debs to a custom cache dir and install with dpkg. First let me confirm writable space and try downloading.",
  "plan": "1. Test writing a larger file (~100MB) to confirm real writable capacity. 2. Create a custom apt cache dir and use `apt-get download` with `Dir::Cache` overridden to fetch the two .deb packages. 3. Install them with dpkg. This avoids apt's refusing space check.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmptest bs=1M count=100 2>&1; rm -f /tmptest\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "mkdir -p /tmp/aptcache && apt-get -o Dir::Cache=/tmp/aptcache update 2>&1 | tail -3\n",
      "duration": 15.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Confirmed: I can write 100MB+ to disk, so real writable space is fine. apt's refusal was just its statvfs disk-check seeing 0 available on the host backing store. Solution: download the .debs directly with `apt-get download` (which doesn't apply the same space guard) and install via dpkg. The needed packages are p7zip-full, libcompress-raw-lzma-perl, and their dependency 7zip.",
  "plan": "Use `apt-get download` to fetch the three .debs into /tmp/aptcache, then install them with `dpkg -i`. After that verify 7z and Compress::Raw::Lzma are available.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cd /tmp/aptcache && apt-get download p7zip-full libcompress-raw-lzma-perl 7zip 2>&1 | tail -10\n",
      "duration": 20.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /tmp/aptcache\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "All three .deb files downloaded successfully into /tmp/aptcache (7zip, libcompress-raw-lzma-perl, p7zip-full). Now I'll install them with dpkg, which doesn't apply apt's disk-space guard.",
  "plan": "Install all three .debs with `dpkg -i`. Then verify 7z is on PATH and Compress::Raw::Lzma loads in perl.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "dpkg -i /tmp/aptcache/*.deb 2>&1 | tail -20\n",
      "duration": 15.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "which 7z; perl -MCompress::Raw::Lzma -e 'print \"LZMA_OK\\n\"' 2>&1\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "7z is installed at /usr/bin/7z and Compress::Raw::Lzma loads fine. Now I can run 7z2john.pl to generate a john hash from secrets.7z, then crack it with john using a 4-digit numeric mask.",
  "plan": "1. Run 7z2john.pl to create a hash file. 2. Inspect the hash. 3. Run john with --mask=?d?d?d?d to crack the 4-digit password. 4. Show the cracked password.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cd /app && perl /app/john/run/7z2john.pl secrets.7z > secrets.hash 2>7z2john.err; cat secrets.hash\n",
      "duration": 1.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat 7z2john.err\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "7z2john.pl produced the hash: secrets.7z:$7z$2$19$0$$16$... in secrets.hash. Now I need to crack it with john using a 4-digit numeric mask (?d?d?d?d).",
  "plan": "Run john with --mask=?d?d?d?d on the hash file. This will try all 10000 combinations. Then show the cracked password with --show.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cd /app && /app/john/run/john --mask=?d?d?d?d secrets.hash 2>&1 | tail -20\n",
      "duration": 20.0
    }
  ]
}

